    <h2 id="validation">Validation test cases</h2>
    <p>
      We tested Honeybee-Revive against the
      <a href="https://github.com/Phius-ResearchComittee/REVIVE/releases/tag/v24.2.0" target="_blank">Phius KMR example model</a>
      for both resiliency simulations and ADORB cost calculations. The test building is a single-zone, single-family home
      with a below-grade basement and two on-grade floor surfaces.
    </p>

    <h3>Resiliency comparison</h3>
    <p>
      We compared peak-week resiliency simulations from the Phius GUI and Honeybee-Revive for both extreme
      cold (winter) and extreme hot (summer) weeks. The comparison tracks indoor dry-bulb temperature,
      relative humidity, SET comfort temperature, outdoor conditions, internal gains, window heat gain and loss,
      and ventilation airflow.
    </p>
    <p><strong>Key findings:</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Indoor dry-bulb, RH, and SET match closely in both winter and summer.</li>
      <li>Outdoor conditions agree within +/- 0.1&deg;C, with minor differences from how each tool processes weather files.</li>
      <li>Internal gains from people, lighting, and equipment match between the two models.</li>
      <li>Window solar gain and heat loss track closely.</li>
    </ul>
    <p><strong>Known differences:</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Kiva ground solver:</strong> The Phius GUI uses Kiva; Honeybee does not support it natively yet.</li>
      <li><strong>Thermal comfort model:</strong> SET output requires the Pierce comfort model, which currently needs a temporary patch in Honeybee.</li>
      <li><strong>Infiltration parameters:</strong> Different temperature and velocity coefficients create a small difference in infiltration airflow.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      <a href="https://github.com/PH-Tools/honeybee_REVIVE_grasshopper/tree/main/tests/resilience/comparison#readme" target="_blank">
        Full resiliency comparison with charts and model files &rarr;
      </a>
    </p>

    <h3>ADORB cost comparison</h3>
    <p>
      We ran full annual EnergyPlus simulations in both the Phius GUI and Honeybee-Revive, then compared ADORB costs.
      The Phius case uses the
      <a href="https://github.com/Phius-ResearchComittee/REVIVE/blob/main/REVIVE2024/adorb.py" target="_blank">Phius GUI ADORB calculator</a>;
      Honeybee-Revive uses <a href="https://github.com/PH-Tools/PH_ADORB" target="_blank">PH-ADORB</a>.
    </p>
    <p><strong>Key findings:</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Indoor conditions, internal gains, and ventilation airflows match closely.</li>
      <li>Summer and winter heating and cooling loads agree across both models.</li>
      <li>Operational energy ADORB costs (energy purchase and CO<sub>2</sub>) match closely.</li>
      <li>Grid transition present-value costs agree between both tools.</li>
    </ul>
    <p><strong>Known differences:</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Mechanical equipment:</strong> The Phius GUI uses hard-coded residential systems, while Honeybee-Revive uses NREL OpenStudio Standards templates, so outputs differ slightly.</li>
      <li><strong>Construction measure costs:</strong> A bug in the Phius GUI double-counts construction material costs at year 0 and inflates ADORB totals. Honeybee-Revive fixes this.</li>
      <li><strong>Year-0 CO<sub>2</sub>:</strong> The Phius GUI leaves out construction material CO<sub>2</sub> at year 0. Honeybee-Revive includes it.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      <a href="https://github.com/PH-Tools/honeybee_REVIVE_grasshopper/tree/main/tests/adorb/comparison#readme" target="_blank">
        Full ADORB cost comparison with charts and model files &rarr;
      </a>
    </p>

